.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt VIRTIO_MEM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtio_mem
.Nd VirtIO memory device driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following lines in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device virtio_mem"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
virtio_mem_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
device driver provides support for VirtIO memory (virtio-mem) devices, the
modern-only VirtIO facility through which a hypervisor dynamically offers
memory to, or reclaims memory from, a running guest.
.Pp
A virtio-mem device exposes a contiguous guest-physical region divided into
fixed-size blocks.
The device tells the driver, through its
.Va requested_size
configuration field, how many bytes it wants
.Em plugged
(made available to the guest); the driver reconciles the plugged amount by
issuing PLUG and UNPLUG requests on the device's request virtqueue and
re-evaluates whenever the device raises a configuration-change interrupt.
The driver implements the complete guest side of that protocol: feature
negotiation, generation-consistent configuration parsing, the
PLUG / UNPLUG / UNPLUG_ALL / STATE request state machine with full response-code
handling, a per-block plugged bitmap, and plugged-size accounting.
.Ss Memory hotplug boundary
The
.Nm
driver negotiates the protocol and plugs and unplugs blocks per the device
contract, but it does
.Em not
online plugged memory into the
.Fx
page allocator.
Unlike Linux, which hands freshly plugged blocks to its memory-hotplug
subsystem,
.Fx
has no supported runtime memory-add path: the physical-segment and
.Vt vm_page
arrays are sized once at boot and cannot be grown at run time.
Consequently plugging memory only consumes host backing without making usable
memory available to the guest.
For this reason the driver defaults to protocol-only operation and does not
issue PLUG requests toward
.Va requested_size
unless explicitly enabled with the
.Va hw.virtio_mem.allow_plug
tunable described below.
The driver is structured so that a future
.Fx
runtime memory-add facility can hook in at a single, clearly marked seam
without changing the protocol core.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
Tunables can be set at the
.Xr loader 8
prompt before booting the kernel or stored in
.Xr loader.conf 5 .
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.virtio_mem.allow_plug
Gate whether the driver issues PLUG requests toward the device's requested
size.
The default value is 0, meaning the driver negotiates, accounts for, and
answers configuration and STATE requests but never plugs memory, because
plugged memory cannot be onlined into the page allocator.
Setting the value to 1 lets the driver exercise the full PLUG / UNPLUG
contract; this is intended for testing the device protocol, not for making
memory available to the guest.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following read-only variables are available as
.Xr sysctl 8
variables, per attached device.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.vtmem. Ns Ar X Ns Va .block_size
The plug/unplug block size in bytes.
.It Va dev.vtmem. Ns Ar X Ns Va .region_size
The total size of the device's guest-physical region in bytes.
.It Va dev.vtmem. Ns Ar X Ns Va .usable_region_size
The size of the currently usable sub-region in bytes.
.It Va dev.vtmem. Ns Ar X Ns Va .requested_size
The number of bytes the device currently requests be plugged.
.It Va dev.vtmem. Ns Ar X Ns Va .plugged_size
The number of bytes currently plugged.
.It Va dev.vtmem. Ns Ar X Ns Va .plugged_blocks
The number of blocks currently plugged.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr virtio 4 ,
.Xr virtio_balloon 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 15.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
driver was written by
.An Kory Heard .
.Sh BUGS
Plugged memory is not onlined into the kernel page allocator, so this driver
cannot yet be used to add usable memory to a running guest; see the
.Sx DESCRIPTION .
